(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-foodcompetition"]={"plain":{"bills":{"foodcompetition":{"slug":"foodcompetition","title":"The Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short title.","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE; FINDINGS; DEFINITIONS","body":"This Act may be cited as the Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE; FINDINGS; DEFINITIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings.","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE; FINDINGS; DEFINITIONS","body":"**(1)** The farm share of a dollar spent on food at home was 18.5 cents in 2024. The remainder is paid to the firms and functions between the farm gate and the checkout, and it is there \u2014 not on the farm \u2014 that the price of food is largely determined.\n\n**(2)** Four firms slaughtered 85 percent of the steers and heifers and 67 percent of the hogs processed in the United States in 2019, against 36 percent and 34 percent in 1980. Four firms sold 80 percent of the corn seed and 75 percent of the soybean seed. The four largest grocery chains held 34 percent of national grocery sales in 2019, against 13 percent in 1990, and in the average rural county the grocery market is concentrated at more than twice the level at which the Federal merger guidelines call a market highly concentrated.\n\n**(3)** Seventy-seven percent of contract broiler growers face no more than three potential integrators, and 54 percent face one or two. A grower who owns the barn and decides nothing that matters cannot bargain over the terms on which the animals in it are placed.\n\n**(4)** Each of the four instruments this Act makes law has existed in some form and has been switched off without any change to a statute. The Department of Agriculture's standard for unfair practices under the Packers and Stockyards Act has been proposed by rule five times since 2010 and is law today in no form. A rule governing tournament payments to poultry growers was finalized in 2025, and its effective date has been delayed to the end of 2027. The Federal Trade Commission filed two actions under the Robinson-Patman Act and dismissed one of them itself within five months. Farmers' right to repair their own equipment exists as a ten-year consent order binding one manufacturer.\n\n**(5)** Whether a grower or producer must prove injury to competition before an unfair practice against the grower or producer is unlawful under the Packers and Stockyards Act has been answered differently by the Department administering that Act and by the courts, and Congress has not spoken to it. This Act states that no such showing is required, because the practices that Act prohibits injure the person subjected to them whether or not they injure a market.\n\n**(6)** A retailer that pays a supplier late, charges the supplier for goods lost in the retailer's own stores, or changes an agreed price after delivery transfers risk and cost to the supplier that the supplier did not price and cannot refuse, because the supplier's remedy is to sue a customer who can end its business. Codes of practice governing that conduct have operated in the United Kingdom since 2010 and across the European Union since 2021. In the United Kingdom the share of suppliers reporting a breach fell from 79 percent in 2014 to 30 percent in 2025. Australia's code was voluntary, carried no penalties, permitted the retailer to contract out of important provisions, and was found by its own reviewer to be ineffective for those reasons before a mandatory code took effect in 2025.\n\n**(7)** The rules of this Act are drafted as duties whose breach a farmer, a grower, a supplier, or a State may establish in court, without any prior finding by an agency and whether or not any agency acts."},{"anchor":"sec-0-3","heading":"\u00a7 0-3. Definitions.","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE; FINDINGS; DEFINITIONS","body":"In this Act:\n\n**(a)** **Agricultural equipment** means equipment primarily designed for use in a farm or ranch operation, including a tractor, trailer, combine, sprayer, tillage implement, baler, irrigation system, and other equipment used to plant, cultivate, irrigate, or harvest agricultural products or to ranch, together with attachments to and repair parts for such equipment. It does not include a self-propelled vehicle designed primarily for the transportation of individuals or property on a street or highway, or an aircraft.\n\n**(b)** **Manufacturer** means a person that manufactures agricultural equipment, or a part or embedded software for it, for sale or lease in commerce, and includes a person that assumes a manufacturer's obligations for a brand of equipment by license, purchase, or succession.\n\n**(c)** **Authorized repair provider** means a person, other than the owner, that provides services for a manufacturer's equipment under an arrangement with the manufacturer granting the person a license to use a trade name, service mark, or other proprietary identifier, or authorization under any other arrangement to act on behalf of the manufacturer. A manufacturer that itself provides services is an authorized repair provider for purposes of comparing terms under \u00a7 1-2.\n\n**(d)** **Independent repair provider** means a person, other than the owner, that provides services for agricultural equipment and is neither an authorized repair provider nor affiliated with one.\n\n**(e)** **Owner** means a person that owns or leases agricultural equipment, and includes a person authorized by the owner to have services performed on it.\n\n**(f)** **Services** means diagnosis, maintenance, repair, and upgrade of agricultural equipment or a part of it; and **maintenance** and **repair** have the meanings given in section 117(d) of title 17, United States Code.\n\n**(g)** **Repair resource** means any of the following that a manufacturer makes available, in any form and on any terms, to an authorized repair provider for the purpose of services: documentation, including a manual, diagram, schematic, reporting output, service code description, library of diagnosed issues, software bill of materials, security code or password, and similar guidance, in any format; a part, including an electronic control unit; embedded software, meaning programmable instructions provided on firmware delivered with or loaded to the equipment, including every patch and fix the manufacturer makes and items described as an operating system, machine code, assembly code, root code, or microcode; firmware; a tool, physical or software, including a tool for reading, clearing, or resetting an electronic fault code, for reprogramming or pairing an electronic component, for restarting equipment after an emissions-related or other electronic shutdown, for resetting an electronic security lock or immobilizer disabled in the course of services, and for performing any of those functions without a network connection where an authorized repair provider can or where \u00a7 1-1(e) requires; a function within \u00a7 1-1(e); and, with the owner's authorization, data arising from the operation of the owner's equipment. A resource whose primary purpose is the sale, marketing, financing, inventory, or warranty administration of an authorized repair provider's business, rather than services, is not a repair resource.\n\n**(h)** **Person** includes an individual, a partnership, a corporation, an association, a cooperative, a trust, an estate, an unincorporated organization, and a government or governmental subdivision or agency.\n\n**(i)** **State** includes the District of Columbia, the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico, and every territory and possession of the United States; and **State law** and **the attorney general of a State** include, respectively, the law and the chief legal officer of an Indian Tribe.\n\n**(j)** **Commerce** means commerce among the several States, or with foreign nations, or in any territory or possession of the United States, or between any such territory or possession and any State or foreign nation, or within the District of Columbia.\n\n**(k)** **Affiliate** means a person that controls, is controlled by, or is under common control with another, and a person that shares officers, directors, employees, offices, or facilities with another; and **control** means the power, by ownership, contract, or otherwise, to direct the management or policies of a person."},{"anchor":"sec-1-1","heading":"\u00a7 1-1. The duty.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE RIGHT TO REPAIR AGRICULTURAL EQUIPMENT","body":"**(a) Parity.** A manufacturer shall make available to every owner and every independent repair provider, for the purpose of services on the manufacturer's agricultural equipment, every repair resource it makes available to any authorized repair provider, on terms no less favorable than the most favorable terms on which it makes that resource available to any authorized repair provider.\n\n**(b) Future resources.** Subsection (a) applies to a repair resource from the day the manufacturer first makes it available to any authorized repair provider, in any market, and a resource released to authorized repair providers in stages is available to owners and independent repair providers from the first stage.\n\n**(b-1) Owner authorization.** A manufacturer may require that an independent repair provider hold the owner's authorization before using a repair resource on a particular piece of equipment, and may require nothing else of the provider as a condition of that use; an authorization may be given by any means and may not be conditioned by the manufacturer on any term.\n\n**(c) Security functions.** With respect to equipment containing an electronic security lock or other security-related function, the repair resources subsection (a) requires include whatever is needed to reset the lock or function when it is disabled in the course of services. A manufacturer may deliver them through a secure release system, and a secure release system that conditions release on anything other than the identity of the owner or independent repair provider and the identity of the equipment, or that verifies either identity by a means more burdensome than the means by which the manufacturer verifies an authorized repair provider, does not satisfy this subsection.\n\n**(d) No software lock on a completed repair.** A manufacturer shall not design, deploy, or maintain any function of agricultural equipment or its embedded software that disables, degrades, or refuses to recognize equipment, a part, or a function by reason of services having been performed, or a part having been installed, by an owner or an independent repair provider rather than by an authorized repair provider. As to equipment in service on the day this title takes effect, a manufacturer satisfies this subsection by making available under subsection (a), as a repair resource, the means by which an owner or independent repair provider clears or overrides any such function.\n\n**(e) Functions the manufacturer performs remotely.** A function of services that a manufacturer, or a person acting for it, performs on agricultural equipment remotely, by network connection or otherwise, for any person, is a repair resource, whether or not a tool for it exists, and subsection (b) applies to it as if the manufacturer's first remote performance of the function for any person were its first making the resource available to an authorized repair provider. As to every such function, the manufacturer shall make available under subsection (a) a means by which an owner or independent repair provider performs the function without the manufacturer's participation and, where technically feasible, without a network connection. Participation means any act of the manufacturer or a person acting for it at the time of performance, other than having supplied the means. Performance without a network connection is technically feasible where the manufacturer or any authorized repair provider performs the function without a network connection on any equipment of the manufacturer in any market, or where the function is one described in \u00a7 0-3(g) as a function of a tool; as to any other function, a manufacturer asserting that performance without a network connection is not technically feasible bears the burden of showing it. Access to the manufacturer's remote performance of the function, on any terms, does not satisfy this subsection.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE I \u2014 THE RIGHT TO REPAIR AGRICULTURAL EQUIPMENT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1-2","heading":"\u00a7 1-2. What \"no less favorable\" means.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE RIGHT TO REPAIR AGRICULTURAL EQUIPMENT","body":"**(a) Price and access.** Terms are no less favorable only where the price to an owner or independent repair provider, computed net of every discount, rebate, and incentive, does not exceed the lowest net price at which the manufacturer makes the resource available to any authorized repair provider; and where the resource is available at the same time, by the same means, and in the same form.\n\n**(b) Terms that are not favorable.** Terms are not favorable, regardless of price, where they impose on an owner or independent repair provider \u2014\n\n**(1)** any obligation to use, or any restriction on the use of, a part, embedded software, firmware, or tool, including a condition that the owner or provider become an authorized repair provider;\n\n**(2)** any requirement that a part, embedded software, firmware, or tool be registered with, paired by, or approved by the manufacturer or an authorized repair provider before it is operational, other than a pairing the owner or independent repair provider can perform with a tool made available under \u00a7 1-1;\n\n**(3)** any requirement to purchase a quantity, a bundle, or a subscription the manufacturer does not require of every authorized repair provider;\n\n**(4)** any waiver, release, or limitation of a right under this Act, or any agreement to arbitrate or to litigate in a forum the owner did not choose; or\n\n**(5)** any condition on the identity, size, location, or business model of the independent repair provider."},{"anchor":"sec-1-3","heading":"\u00a7 1-3. Trade secrets; safety; emissions.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE RIGHT TO REPAIR AGRICULTURAL EQUIPMENT","body":"**(a) Trade secrets.** Nothing in this title requires a manufacturer to disclose the composition, design, or method of development of a repair resource where that information is a trade secret and the usability of the resource for services is not diminished by withholding it. **A manufacturer shall not refuse to make a repair resource available, or condition its availability, on the ground that the resource itself is a trade secret; and no other ground of confidentiality, trade secrecy, copyright, patent, trademark, contract, or intellectual property excuses a duty under this title, the omission of any such ground being a determination of Congress.**\n\n**(b) What this title does not authorize.** Nothing in this title authorizes an owner or independent repair provider to modify agricultural equipment so as to deactivate a safety notification system, to bring the equipment out of compliance with any applicable Federal, State, or local safety or emissions law, or to evade any law governing emissions, copyright, trademark, or patent; and services necessary to restore equipment to compliance, or to restore its function after an emissions-related shutdown, are not a modification within this subsection. **This subsection limits what a repairer may do. It does not limit what a manufacturer must provide, and a manufacturer shall not withhold a repair resource on the ground that the resource could be misused.**\n\n**(c) Liability for repairs.** A manufacturer, and an authorized repair provider, is not liable for a faulty or improper repair performed by an owner or independent repair provider, or for damage, loss of use, or reduced functionality resulting from such a repair, and is not liable to any person for an action taken while using or relying on data provided in compliance with this title.\n\n**(d) Circumvention for services.** Notwithstanding subsections (a)(1), (a)(2), and (b) of section 1201 of title 17, United States Code, an owner or independent repair provider, and a person acting on the owner's or provider's behalf, may circumvent a technological measure that controls access to, or protects a right of a copyright owner in, a work contained in agricultural equipment or in a repair resource, and a person may make, import, offer, provide, or otherwise traffic in a technology, product, service, device, component, or part for that purpose, where the purpose of the circumvention is to perform services on the equipment, to enable interoperability of a part or repair resource with it, or to conduct security research relating to it. This subsection does not depend on any exemption adopted under section 1201(a)(1)(C) of that title, and the expiration or non-renewal of such an exemption does not affect it."},{"anchor":"sec-1-4","heading":"\u00a7 1-4. Void terms; no contracting out.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE RIGHT TO REPAIR AGRICULTURAL EQUIPMENT","body":"**(a)** A term of any contract, license, warranty, agreement, or arrangement, whenever entered, that waives, avoids, restricts, limits, or conditions a manufacturer's duty under this title, or an owner's or independent repair provider's right under it, is void and unenforceable, and the remainder of the instrument is enforceable without it.\n\n**(b)** A memorandum of understanding, industry commitment, or other arrangement between a manufacturer and any person concerning the repair of agricultural equipment does not satisfy, reduce, or displace any duty under this title, and where it would deny an owner or independent repair provider anything this title provides, this title governs.\n\n**(c)** A warranty on agricultural equipment is not voided, and coverage under it is not reduced, by reason of services having been performed by an owner or independent repair provider, or by reason of a part not supplied by the manufacturer having been used, except to the extent the manufacturer establishes that the services or part caused the defect for which warranty coverage is claimed. This subsection applies whether or not the equipment is a consumer product within the meaning of section 101 of the Magnuson-Moss Warranty\u2014Federal Trade Commission Improvement Act (15 U.S.C. 2301), supplements section 102(c) of that Act (15 U.S.C. 2302(c)) where that section applies, and is not subject to waiver by the Federal Trade Commission under that section or otherwise."},{"anchor":"sec-1-5","heading":"\u00a7 1-5. Retaliation.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE RIGHT TO REPAIR AGRICULTURAL EQUIPMENT","body":"**(a)** A manufacturer shall not, and shall not cause, permit, or induce an authorized repair provider or dealer to, refuse to sell, refuse to service, delay, degrade, surcharge, or otherwise treat less favorably any owner or independent repair provider by reason of the owner's or provider's performance of services, purchase or use of a repair resource under this title, or assertion of any right under this title. Treatment is less favorable if it would tend to deter a reasonable owner or provider from doing a thing stated in this subsection.\n\n**(b)** Title VII governs enforcement of this section, and the causal standard and defenses stated in \u00a7 7-4 apply."},{"anchor":"sec-1-6","heading":"\u00a7 1-6. Relationship to State law.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE RIGHT TO REPAIR AGRICULTURAL EQUIPMENT","body":"**(a)** This title establishes a floor. It does not preempt, displace, or limit a law of a State that provides an owner or independent repair provider a right, a remedy, or a term more favorable than this title, and such a law governs to that extent.\n\n**(b)** Where a law of a State provides, by its own terms, that it is repealed or ceases to apply upon the enactment of Federal legislation establishing a right to repair agricultural equipment, every right, duty, definition, and remedy that law provided on the day before the date of enactment of this Act is, as to agricultural equipment in that State, a right, duty, definition, and remedy under this title from the date of enactment, to the extent it is more favorable to an owner or independent repair provider than this title, and it is enforceable under Title VII as a provision of this title."},{"anchor":"sec-1-7","heading":"\u00a7 1-7. Severability within this title.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE RIGHT TO REPAIR AGRICULTURAL EQUIPMENT","body":"Section 1-1(d) is severable from \u00a7 1-1(a). Section 1-6(b) is severable from \u00a7 1-6(a). The invalidity of any subsection of this title does not affect any other subsection of this title or any other provision of this Act, and no court shall treat the subsections of this title as an integrated scheme."},{"anchor":"sec-2-1","heading":"\u00a7 2-1. Definitions for this title.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PACKERS AND STOCKYARDS","body":"In this title, **packer**, **swine contractor**, **live poultry dealer**, **poultry grower**, **poultry growing arrangement**, **swine production contract**, and **swine production contract grower** have the meanings given in section 2 of the Packers and Stockyards Act, 1921 (7 U.S.C. 182); **the Act** means that Act; and **grower** means a poultry grower, a swine production contract grower, or a person that sells livestock or poultry to, or raises livestock or poultry under contract for, a packer, swine contractor, or live poultry dealer.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE II \u2014 PACKERS AND STOCKYARDS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-2-2","heading":"\u00a7 2-2. Injury to competition is not an element.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PACKERS AND STOCKYARDS","body":"**(a) Amendment.** Section 202 of the Act (7 U.S.C. 192) is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **A practice or device is unfair, unjustly discriminatory, or deceptive within subdivision (a), and a preference, advantage, prejudice, or disadvantage is undue or unreasonable within subdivision (b), by reason of its effect on the person subjected to it, and an effect is any deprivation, cost, risk, delay, or disadvantage to the person, whether or not measurable in money. No showing of injury or likely injury to competition, of harm to competition, of market power, of a relevant market, or of an anticompetitive purpose or effect is an element of a violation of subdivision (a) or (b), a condition of any order or remedy for such a violation, or a defense to it. Subdivisions (a) and (b) are statutory duties whose content does not depend on the construction given to any antitrust law or to any other statute.**\n>\n> **A practice or device is unfair within subdivision (a) if, among other things, it \u2014 (1) deprives a person of a benefit that the person's arrangement with the packer, swine contractor, or live poultry dealer provides; (2) transfers to the person a cost or risk that the arrangement does not allocate to the person; (3) conditions the arrangement, or its continuation, renewal, placements, or terms, on the person's forgoing or not asserting a right under this Act or any other law, or on the person's silence concerning the arrangement; (4) is retaliation within section 211; (5) is a practice stated in section 212; or (6) makes, modifies, performs, enforces, terminates, or refuses a contract or arrangement with the person by employing a false or misleading statement, or an omission of material information necessary to make a statement not misleading. This paragraph is illustrative and not limiting, and the omission of a practice from it is not a determination that the practice is fair.**\n\n**(b) Application.** The amendment made by subsection (a) applies to conduct occurring after the date of enactment of this Act, in any proceeding commenced before or after that date."},{"anchor":"sec-2-3","heading":"\u00a7 2-3. Retaliation.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PACKERS AND STOCKYARDS","body":"The Act is amended by inserting after section 210 (7 U.S.C. 197c) the following:\n\n> **SEC. 211. RETALIATION.**\n>\n> **(a)** No packer, swine contractor, or live poultry dealer shall terminate, decline to renew, suspend, reduce the placements, deliveries, or payments under, alter the terms of, or otherwise take or threaten an action adverse to a person under a poultry growing arrangement, a swine production contract, or a contract for the purchase of livestock or poultry, because the person has asserted or attempted to assert a right under this Act, under the arrangement or contract, or under any other law; has provided information to, or communicated with, any officer of the United States, of a State, or of an Indian Tribe, a Member of Congress, a member of a State legislature, a court, or the press concerning the arrangement or the conduct of the packer, swine contractor, or live poultry dealer, or has refused a request to make such a communication that no law requires; has joined, formed, or assisted, or refused to join, an association, organization, or cooperative of growers or producers, or has processed, prepared for market, handled, or marketed livestock or poultry collectively; has communicated or cooperated with any person for the purpose of improving the production or marketing of livestock or poultry; has communicated, negotiated, or contracted with any other person for the purpose of exploring or entering a business relationship; has supported or participated as a witness in any proceeding; or is named or identifiable in any proceeding under this Act. An action is adverse if it would tend to deter a reasonable person from doing a thing stated in this subsection.\n>\n> **(a-1)** This section states in statute, and does not narrow, the protection that section 201.304(b) of title 9, Code of Federal Regulations, as in effect on January 1, 2025, provides; that section continues to apply according to its terms whether or not it remains in effect, and no rule may narrow this section.\n>\n> **(b)** A violation of subsection (a) is established where a thing stated in that subsection was a contributing factor in the action, and no other causal standard applies. It is a defense only that the same action would have been taken had the person done none of the things stated in subsection (a), and the packer, swine contractor, or live poultry dealer bears the burden of establishing that defense by clear and convincing evidence.\n>\n> **(c)** A violation of subsection (a) is an unfair practice within section 202(a), and a person aggrieved by it recovers, in addition to any other relief, the relief necessary to be made whole, including restoration of the arrangement, the placements, and the terms that were withdrawn or altered."},{"anchor":"sec-2-4","heading":"\u00a7 2-4. Grower payment systems and capital investments.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PACKERS AND STOCKYARDS","body":"The Act is further amended by inserting after section 211, as added by \u00a7 2-3, the following:\n\n> **SEC. 212. GROWER PAYMENT SYSTEMS AND CAPITAL INVESTMENTS.**\n>\n> **(a) Definitions.** In this section \u2014 **complex** means a group of local facilities under the common management of a live poultry dealer, including any hatchery, feed mill, slaughtering facility, or processing facility; **ranking system** means any arrangement under which a poultry grower's compensation depends in any part on a grouping, ranking, or comparison of poultry growers delivering poultry during a period; **gross payments** means the total compensation a poultry grower receives from a live poultry dealer, including base payments, housing, energy, square-footage, lay-out, equipment, bonus, capital-investment, and litter payments, before any deduction or assignment; and **additional capital investment** means an amount at or above the amount set in the schedule to the Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act, per structure, paid by a poultry grower or swine production contract grower over the life of an arrangement beyond the initial investment in facilities, including upgrades to structures and equipment and the goods and services directly attributable to them, and excluding maintenance and repair.\n>\n> **(b) Limit on ranked payments.** A live poultry dealer shall not, in any calendar year, make payments to poultry growers at a complex under a ranking system in an aggregate amount exceeding the percentage set in the schedule to the Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act of total gross payments to poultry growers at that complex in that year. The amount paid under a ranking system is the portion of each grower's gross payments that would have differed had the grower's grouping, ranking, or comparison differed, and a payment is under a ranking system whatever it is called. The amount by which payments under a ranking system exceed the limit is the measure of a violation of this subsection, and a dealer whose payments exceed it violates this subsection only to that extent.\n>\n> **(c) No reduction by ranking.** A live poultry dealer shall not use a poultry grower's grouping, ranking, or comparison to others to reduce any rate of compensation stated in the grower's arrangement, or to reduce the number, size, or frequency of the flocks placed with the grower.\n>\n> **(d) Fair comparison.** A live poultry dealer that compensates poultry growers under a ranking system shall, before computing any grower's compensation for a period, adjust the comparison to remove differences in performance attributable to the quality or quantity of the inputs the dealer supplied to each grower, to the production practices the dealer assigned to each grower, and to the composition of the group in which each grower was compared; shall state in writing, and provide to each grower on request, the method by which it makes those adjustments, the period over which it evaluates the comparison, the circumstances in which it removes a grower from a comparison, and the means by which a grower's concern about the comparison is resolved and within what time; and shall compensate a grower for whom a fair comparison cannot be made for a period by a method stated in the arrangement that does not depend on comparison and that yields not less than the grower would have received at the complex average. A comparison for which the writing this subsection requires does not exist is not a fair comparison, and a writing from which the adjustment made for a grower cannot be reproduced from the dealer's records of the inputs supplied, the practices assigned, and the group composition is not the writing this subsection requires; in either case the grower's compensation for the period is the greater of the amount paid and the complex average.\n>\n> **(e) Capital investment disclosure.** A live poultry dealer or swine contractor that requests or requires an additional capital investment shall first provide the grower a written disclosure stating the purpose of the investment for the dealer and for the grower and the research and other material the dealer relied on; every financial incentive and payment to the grower associated with it; the construction schedule; the housing specifications; every required or approved manufacturer or vendor, and every financial benefit the dealer, or any officer, director, decision-making employee, or family member of one, receives from the use of that manufacturer or vendor; and an analysis of the returns the grower can expect, with the assumptions, risks, and uncertainties, sufficient for the grower to make the grower's own projection. A grower is not bound to make, and no arrangement may be terminated, not renewed, or reduced by reason of the grower's not making, an additional capital investment for which this disclosure was not provided; and a disclosure that omits any item this subsection states is not a disclosure under it.\n>\n> **(f) Records.** A live poultry dealer or swine contractor shall retain every record relevant to its compliance with this section for not less than five years and shall produce it on request to the grower to whom it relates and to a plaintiff in an action under section 308.\n>\n> **(g)** A violation of this section is an unfair practice within section 202(a)."},{"anchor":"sec-2-5","heading":"\u00a7 2-5. Liability and remedies.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PACKERS AND STOCKYARDS","body":"Section 308 of the Act (7 U.S.C. 209) is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **SEC. 308. LIABILITY TO INDIVIDUALS FOR VIOLATIONS; ENFORCEMENT GENERALLY.**\n>\n> **(a)** If any person subject to this Act violates any of the provisions of this Act, or of any order of the Secretary under this Act, relating to the purchase, sale, or handling of livestock, the purchase or sale of poultry, or relating to any poultry growing arrangement or swine production contract, that person shall be liable to the person or persons injured thereby for the full amount of damages sustained in consequence of such violation, **and for the amount set in the schedule to the Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act for each violation, and the court shall award to a prevailing plaintiff reasonable attorney's fees, expert fees, and costs.**\n>\n> **(b)** Such liability may be enforced either (1) by complaint to the Secretary as provided in section 309, or (2) by suit in any district court of the United States of competent jurisdiction **or in any State court of competent jurisdiction, and a suit under this section is an action under the Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act for purposes of Title VII of that Act, which governs its forum, pleading, defenses, and construction**; but this section shall not in any way abridge or alter the remedies now existing at common law or by statute, but the provisions of this Act are in addition to such remedies.\n>\n> **(c) No precondition; no channeling.** Neither a complaint to the Secretary, nor any finding, order, rule, or inaction of the Secretary, is a condition of a suit under subsection (b)(2), a defense to it, or evidence in it, and a proceeding under section 203 or 309 does not stay, preclude, or displace a suit under subsection (b)(2)."},{"anchor":"sec-2-6","heading":"\u00a7 2-6. Arbitration.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PACKERS AND STOCKYARDS","body":"Section 210 of the Act (7 U.S.C. 197c) is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **SEC. 210. ARBITRATION.**\n>\n> **(a)** Notwithstanding chapter 1 of title 9, United States Code, at the election of the producer or grower, or of the named representative of a class or collective action, no predispute arbitration agreement and no predispute joint-action waiver is valid or enforceable with respect to the case relating to a controversy arising under a livestock or poultry contract or under this Act, and **case** means the entire civil proceeding, not the claim alone. The election may be made after the controversy arises, and a term purporting to require the election earlier, or to waive it, is void.\n>\n> **(b)** A court shall not compel arbitration of any part of such a case unless it first determines that subsection (a) does not apply, notwithstanding any delegation of that determination to an arbitrator and notwithstanding any incorporation of arbitral rules.\n>\n> **(c)** The election is available where the claim is pleaded nonfrivolously, and no more is required to invoke it. Where any provision of an agreement is unenforceable under this section, the agreement is unenforceable in its entirety as to that case, notwithstanding any severability term in it. An agreement is **predispute** if entered before the conduct giving rise to the controversy occurred, whenever the claim is filed.\n>\n> **(d)** A producer or grower may, after a controversy arises, consent in writing to arbitrate it, and nothing in this section limits that consent. In this section, a **livestock or poultry contract** means a poultry growing arrangement, a swine production contract, and any contract for the purchase or sale of livestock or poultry to which a packer, swine contractor, or live poultry dealer is a party.\n>\n> **(e)** Any action by or on behalf of a packer, swine contractor, or live poultry dealer that violates this section, including any action that has the intent or effect of limiting the ability of a producer or grower freely to make an election under subsection (a), is an unfair practice under this Act.\n>\n> **(f)** This section applies to any contract entered into, amended, altered, modified, renewed, or extended before, on, or after the date of enactment of the Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act."},{"anchor":"sec-2-7","heading":"\u00a7 2-7. Civil penalties determined by a court.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PACKERS AND STOCKYARDS","body":"**(a)** Section 203(b) of the Act (7 U.S.C. 193(b)) is amended by striking \"The Secretary may also assess a civil penalty of not more than $10,000 for each such violation. In determining the amount of the civil penalty to be assessed under this section, the Secretary shall consider the gravity of the offense, the size of the business involved, and the effect of the penalty on the person's ability to continue in business. If, after the lapse of the period allowed for appeal or after the affirmance of such penalty, the person against whom the civil penalty is assessed fails to pay such penalty, the Secretary may refer the matter to the Attorney General who may recover such penalty by an action in the appropriate district court of the United States.\" and inserting **\"A packer or swine contractor that has violated any provision of this title is liable to the United States for a civil penalty of not more than the amount set in the schedule to the Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act for each such violation, which the Attorney General, on referral by the Secretary or on the Attorney General's own motion, recovers by an action in the appropriate district court of the United States, and which the court determines, considering the gravity of the offense, the size of the business involved, and the effect of the penalty on the person's ability to continue in business.\"**\n\n**(b)** Section 312(b) of the Act (7 U.S.C. 213(b)) is amended by striking \"The Secretary may also assess a civil penalty of not more than $10,000 for each such violation. In determining the amount of the civil penalty to be assessed under this section, the Secretary shall consider the gravity of the offense, the size of the business involved, and the effect of the penalty on the person's ability to continue in business. If, after the lapse of the period allowed for appeal or after the affirmance of such penalty, the person against whom the civil penalty is assessed fails to pay such penalty, the Secretary may refer the matter to the Attorney General who may recover such penalty by an action in the appropriate district court of the United States.\" and inserting **\"A stockyard owner, market agency, or dealer that has violated subsection (a) is liable to the United States for a civil penalty of not more than the amount set in the schedule to the Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act for each such violation, which the Attorney General, on referral by the Secretary or on the Attorney General's own motion, recovers by an action in the appropriate district court of the United States, and which the court determines, considering the gravity of the offense, the size of the business involved, and the effect of the penalty on the person's ability to continue in business.\"**\n\n**(b-1)** Section 411(b) of the Act (7 U.S.C. 228b\u20132(b)) is amended by striking \"The Secretary may also assess a civil penalty of not more than $20,000 for each such violation. In determining the amount of the civil penalty to be assessed under this section, the Secretary shall consider the gravity of the offense, the size of the business involved, and the effect of the penalty on the person's ability to continue in business: Provided, however, That in no event can the penalty assessed by the Secretary take priority over or impede the ability of the live poultry dealer to pay any unpaid cash seller or poultry grower. If, after the lapse of the period allowed for appeal or after the affirmance of such penalty, the person against whom the civil penalty is assessed fails to pay such penalty, the Secretary may refer the matter to the Attorney General, who may recover such penalty by an action in the appropriate District Court of the United States.\" and inserting **\"A live poultry dealer that has violated any provision of section 207 or 410 is liable to the United States for a civil penalty of not more than the amount set in the schedule to the Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act for each such violation, which the Attorney General, on referral by the Secretary or on the Attorney General's own motion, recovers by an action in the appropriate district court of the United States, and which the court determines, considering the gravity of the offense, the size of the business involved, and the effect of the penalty on the person's ability to continue in business; and in no event may the penalty take priority over or impede the ability of the live poultry dealer to pay any unpaid cash seller or poultry grower.\"**\n\n**(b-2)** Every civil penalty under the Act is determined by a district court, and none is assessed by the Secretary.\n\n**(c)** Nothing in this section limits the Secretary's authority to issue an order to cease and desist under section 203, 312, or 411 of the Act, or the review of such an order under section 204."},{"anchor":"sec-2-8","heading":"\u00a7 2-8. Severability within this title.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 PACKERS AND STOCKYARDS","body":"Each section of this title is severable from every other. The second paragraph added by \u00a7 2-2(a) is severable from the first. The invalidity of any provision of this title does not affect any other provision of this title or of this Act, and no court shall treat the provisions of this title as an integrated scheme."},{"anchor":"sec-3-1","heading":"\u00a7 3-1. Definitions for this title.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 COMMODITY PROMOTION PROGRAMS","body":"**(a)** **Commodity promotion law** has the meaning given in section 501(a) of the Federal Agriculture Improvement and Reform Act of 1996 (7 U.S.C. 7401(a)), and includes the Commodity Promotion, Research, and Information Act of 1996 (7 U.S.C. 7411 et seq.) and each Act that section names.\n\n**(b)** **Board** means a board, committee, council, or other body that administers an order, or develops or approves plans, projects, or budgets, under a commodity promotion law, and includes the Cattlemen's Beef Promotion and Research Board and the Beef Promotion Operating Committee, the National Pork Board, the Egg Board, and the National Dairy Promotion and Research Board.\n\n**(c)** **Assessment funds** means every amount collected under a commodity promotion law, the earnings on it, and every amount derived from it.\n\n**(d)** **Lobbying** means influencing legislation within the meaning of section 4911(d) and (e)(2) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986; influencing governmental action or policy of the United States, a State, or a foreign government, other than recommending to the Secretary of Agriculture an amendment to an order; and any activity for which a person is required to register under the Lobbying Disclosure Act of 1995 or a corresponding law of a State. A person **engages in lobbying** if the person, or an affiliate of the person, does any of those things or employs or retains a person to do them.\n\n**(e)** **Affiliate** has the meaning given in \u00a7 0-3(k).","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE III \u2014 COMMODITY PROMOTION PROGRAMS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-3-2","heading":"\u00a7 3-2. No assessment funds to persons that lobby.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 COMMODITY PROMOTION PROGRAMS","body":"**(a) The rule.** A board shall not enter into or perform a contract or agreement with, pay assessment funds to, distribute assessment funds to, share employees, offices, or facilities with, or accept services from, a person that engages in lobbying. A contract, agreement, or distribution that this subsection prohibits is void, and assessment funds paid under it are recoverable by the board and, if the board does not recover them within one year, by the Secretary of Agriculture or by any person paying assessments, for the board.\n\n**(a-0) Certification.** Before entering into or performing a contract or agreement with, or paying or distributing assessment funds to, any person, a board shall obtain from the person a written certification, signed under penalty of perjury, that neither the person nor any affiliate of the person engages in lobbying, and shall renew it annually. A board that holds a current certification has complied with subsection (a) as to that person unless it knew or had reason to know the certification was false; a person that gives a false certification is liable to the board for every amount received under it and for the amount set in the schedule to this Act, and section 1001 of title 18, United States Code, applies to the certification.\n\n**(a-1) Collection is not a service.** The collection and remittance of assessments by a person that a commodity promotion law designates or permits to collect them, including a qualified State beef council under section 5(8) of the Beef Promotion and Research Act of 1985 (7 U.S.C. 2904(8)), is not a service accepted, and a credit a producer receives for a contribution to such a person is not a distribution, within subsection (a); but no assessment funds may be retained by, or paid to, such a person for the collection beyond the actual cost of it.\n\n**(a-2) Separation \u2014 the fallback where subsection (a) does not apply.** Where, and to the extent that, subsection (a) is held invalid or is not in force, a board shall not enter into or perform a contract or agreement with, pay or distribute assessment funds to, or accept services from, a person that engages in lobbying unless the person performs the contract, receives the funds, and provides the services through an affiliate that is separately incorporated; has no officer, director, or employee in common with any person that engages in lobbying; occupies separate offices; keeps separate books and accounts audited separately; and receives no funds, services, or facilities from, and provides none to, any person that engages in lobbying, other than the contract itself. A person that does not satisfy every condition of this subsection is, for purposes of it, a person that engages in lobbying. **Subsection (a) and this subsection are severable from each other; this subsection operates whether or not subsection (a) is in force; and no court shall treat them as an integrated scheme.**\n\n**(b) Conforming amendments.** **(1)** Section 515(f)(1) of the Commodity Promotion, Research, and Information Act of 1996 (7 U.S.C. 7414(f)(1)) is amended by adding at the end the following flush sentence: \"**No contract or agreement under subparagraph (A) may be entered into or performed with, and no amount under subparagraph (B) may be paid or distributed to, a person that engages in lobbying within the meaning of section 3-1(d) of the Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act.**\". **(2)** Section 5(6) of the Beef Promotion and Research Act of 1985 (7 U.S.C. 2904(6)) is amended by striking \"with established national nonprofit industry-governed organizations, including the federation referred to in paragraph (4),\" and inserting \"**with persons that do not engage in lobbying within the meaning of section 3-1(d) of the Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act**\". **(3)** Section 1620(c) of the Pork Promotion, Research, and Consumer Information Act of 1985 (7 U.S.C. 4809(c)) is amended by adding at the end the following: \"**(5) No amount under this subsection may be distributed to a State association, or to any other person, that engages in lobbying within the meaning of section 3-1(d) of the Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act.**\"\n\n**(c) Transition.** A contract or agreement in force on the date of enactment that subsection (a) would prohibit terminates on the last day of the twelfth month after enactment, and no amount may be paid under it after that day."},{"anchor":"sec-3-3","heading":"\u00a7 3-3. Publication.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 COMMODITY PROMOTION PROGRAMS","body":"**(a) The duty.** Within sixty days after the end of each fiscal year, each board shall publish, and the Secretary of Agriculture shall post on a single public site, for that year: the budget approved for the board; every contract and agreement of the board, with the counterparty, the amount, and the purpose; every expenditure of assessment funds above the amount set in the schedule to this Act, by payee and purpose; the audit under section 515(g)(2) of the Commodity Promotion, Research, and Information Act of 1996 (7 U.S.C. 7414(g)(2)) or the corresponding provision of the commodity promotion law; the independent evaluation under section 515(h) of that Act (7 U.S.C. 7414(h)) and section 501(c) of the Federal Agriculture Improvement and Reform Act of 1996 (7 U.S.C. 7401(c)); the minutes of every meeting of the board; and the compensation of each employee of the board above the amount set in the schedule to this Act. A board's publication on its own site satisfies its duty under this subsection whether or not the Secretary has posted it, and the Secretary's failure to post does not excuse a board.\n\n**(b) Conforming amendment.** Section 515(i)(4) of the Commodity Promotion, Research, and Information Act of 1996 (7 U.S.C. 7414(i)(4)) is amended by adding at the end the following: \"**(E) Publication under section 3-3 of the Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act.** Nothing in this paragraph, or in any corresponding provision of a commodity promotion law, prevents or excuses a publication that section 3-3 of the Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act requires, and information that section requires to be published is not confidential.\"\n\n**(b-1) Relationship.** Subsection (a) fixes the time and the place of publications that section 501(c) of the Federal Agriculture Improvement and Reform Act of 1996 (7 U.S.C. 7401(c)) and section 515(g)(2) of the Commodity Promotion, Research, and Information Act of 1996 already require to be made or submitted, and adds the remainder; it does not displace either.\n\n**(c) Enforcement.** The duty of subsection (a) is ministerial. A person paying an assessment under a commodity promotion law, and any association of such persons, is aggrieved under \u00a7 7-1 by a failure to publish, and the court shall order publication. The denial of a publication under this section to a person compelled to pay assessments is an injury to that person's interest in the use of the assessments, of the kind a beneficiary suffers when denied an accounting, and the person need show no other injury."},{"anchor":"sec-3-4","heading":"\u00a7 3-4. No disparagement of any food.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 COMMODITY PROMOTION PROGRAMS","body":"**(a) The rule.** No promotion, advertising, research, information, or other activity paid for in any part with assessment funds may disparage any food or agricultural product, including a product offered as a substitute for the commodity the assessment funds promote, or make a claim concerning a competing or substitute product that is false or misleading. An activity **disparages** a product if it states or implies that the product is unsafe, unhealthy, inferior, unnatural, or unfit, or compares it unfavorably to the promoted commodity, other than by stating a specific fact that is true, not misleading, and attributed to its source.\n\n**(b) Conforming amendments.** Section 5 of the Beef Promotion and Research Act of 1985 (7 U.S.C. 2904) is amended by adding at the end the following: \"**(13) The order shall prohibit any promotion, advertising, research, information, or other activity paid for in any part with funds collected by the Board from disparaging any food or agricultural product, including a product offered as a substitute for beef, or from making a claim concerning a competing or substitute product that is false or misleading.**\" Section 8 of the Egg Research and Consumer Information Act (7 U.S.C. 2707) is amended by adding at the end the following: \"**(k) Providing that no promotion, advertising, research, information, or other activity paid for in any part with funds collected by the Egg Board shall disparage any food or agricultural product, including a product offered as a substitute for eggs, or make a claim concerning a competing or substitute product that is false or misleading.**\""},{"anchor":"sec-3-5","heading":"\u00a7 3-5. Government control preserved.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 COMMODITY PROMOTION PROGRAMS","body":"Nothing in this title reduces, transfers, or conditions the authority of the Secretary of Agriculture to approve or disapprove any plan, project, budget, contract, or agreement under a commodity promotion law, and every promotion, advertising, and information activity paid for with assessment funds continues to be developed under the Secretary's approval and to be the message of the United States. This title reaches how assessment funds may be spent and what must be published; it confers no authority on any board or any producer over the content of any message."},{"anchor":"sec-3-6","heading":"\u00a7 3-6. Civil penalties determined by a court.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 COMMODITY PROMOTION PROGRAMS","body":"Section 520 of the Commodity Promotion, Research, and Information Act of 1996 (7 U.S.C. 7419) is amended \u2014 **(1)** in subsection (c)(1), by striking \"may be assessed by the Secretary a civil penalty\" and inserting \"**is liable to the United States for a civil penalty, which a district court of the United States determines in an action the Attorney General brings on referral by the Secretary or on the Attorney General's own motion,**\"; **(2)** in subsection (c)(4), by striking \"No order assessing a penalty or cease-and-desist order\" and inserting \"**No cease-and-desist order**\"; **(3)** in subsection (c)(5), by striking \"An order assessing a penalty or a cease-and-desist order\" and inserting \"**A cease-and-desist order**\"; **(4)** in subsection (d)(1), by striking \"an order is issued under subsection (c)\" and inserting \"**a cease-and-desist order is issued under subsection (c)(3)**\"; **(5)** in subsection (e), by striking \"a civil penalty assessed by the Secretary\" and inserting \"**a civil penalty, which a district court determines in the same manner,**\"; and **(6)** by striking subsection (f) and inserting \"**(f) Every civil penalty under this section is determined by a district court, and none is assessed by the Secretary.**\""},{"anchor":"sec-3-7","heading":"\u00a7 3-7. Severability within this title.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 COMMODITY PROMOTION PROGRAMS","body":"Sections 3-2, 3-3, 3-4, and 3-6 are severable from one another, and within \u00a7 3-2, subsection (a) and subsection (a-2) are severable from each other as that section states. Section 3-5 is a rule of construction that applies to whatever remains. The invalidity of any provision of this title does not affect any other provision of this title or of this Act, and no court shall treat the provisions of this title as an integrated scheme."},{"anchor":"sec-4-1","heading":"\u00a7 4-1. Definitions for this title.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 GROCERY SUPPLY PRACTICES","body":"**(a)** **Agricultural and food products** means products of agriculture, and products processed for use as food or drink, and includes beverages.\n\n**(b)** **Covered buyer** means a person whose annual sales of agricultural and food products at retail in the United States, or whose annual purchases of agricultural and food products for resale in the United States, exceed the amount set in the schedule to this Act; and persons treated as a single employer under subsection (b) or (c) of section 414 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 are one person, and their sales and purchases are aggregated. The threshold is a step: a person above it is a covered buyer as to every supply agreement, and a person below it is not.\n\n**(c)** **Supplier** means a person that sells agricultural and food products to a covered buyer for resale, directly or through one or more intermediaries, and includes an agricultural producer that sells to an intermediary that sells to the covered buyer; it does not include an affiliate of the covered buyer.\n\n**(d)** **Supply agreement** means every agreement between a covered buyer and a supplier for the supply of agricultural and food products, together with every subsequent agreement, arrangement, or understanding made under, pursuant to, or in relation to it, whether or not in writing.\n\n**(e)** A covered buyer **requires** an action of a supplier where the supplier does not agree to the action, whether or not in response to a request or suggestion of the buyer, otherwise than in response to ordinary commercial pressure. Pressure attributable in whole or part to the covered buyer is ordinary commercial pressure only where it involves no duress, including economic duress; is objectively justifiable; is transparent; and results in like cases being treated alike. The covered buyer bears the burden of establishing that an action was not required.\n\n**(f)** **Payment** means any compensation or inducement in any form, monetary or otherwise, and includes more favorable contractual terms and the amount by which a charge for a service, or a purchase, from the covered buyer or an affiliate of it exceeds its fair value.\n\n**(g)** **Promotion** means an offer for sale at an introductory or reduced retail price, whether or not with another benefit to consumers, intended to subsist only for a specified period.\n\n**(h)** **Shrinkage** means loss of agricultural and food products after delivery to the covered buyer's premises, arising from theft, loss, or accounting error; and **wastage** means agricultural and food products that become unfit for sale after delivery to the covered buyer.\n\n**(i)** To **delist** a supplier is to cease to purchase agricultural and food products from the supplier, or to reduce the volume purchased from the supplier significantly relative to the volume that supplier supplied to the covered buyer.\n\n**(j)** **Reasonable notice** means notice whose length is reasonable in light of the duration of the supply agreement or the frequency of orders under it; the characteristics of the products, including durability and seasonality; the value of the affected orders relative to the supplier's turnover; and the foreseeable effect of the notice on the supplier's business. Notice of less than the period set in the schedule to this Act is not reasonable notice.\n\n**(k)** **Perishable** describes agricultural and food products that by their nature or stage of processing are liable to become unfit for sale within thirty days after harvest, production, or processing.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IV \u2014 GROCERY SUPPLY PRACTICES","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-4-2","heading":"\u00a7 4-2. Prohibited practices.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 GROCERY SUPPLY PRACTICES","body":"A covered buyer shall not \u2014\n\n**(1)** vary a supply agreement retrospectively, or require a supplier to consent to a retrospective variation, except by an adjustment whose triggering circumstance, outside the buyer's control, and whose method of calculation the supply agreement stated clearly and unambiguously before the circumstance arose;\n\n**(2)** unilaterally change a term of a supply agreement concerning the frequency, method, place, timing, or volume of supply or delivery, the quality standards, the terms of payment, or the price;\n\n**(3)** pay a supplier for products delivered later than thirty days, for perishable products, or sixty days, for other products, after the later of the date of delivery, or the end of the agreed delivery period in which delivery was made, and the date on which the amount payable is set; and where the covered buyer sets the amount payable, or pays on the sale of the products rather than on their delivery, the period runs from delivery;\n\n**(4)** cancel an order of perishable products on less than thirty days' notice;\n\n**(5)** require a payment from a supplier that is not related to the sale of the supplier's products;\n\n**(6)** require a payment from a supplier as compensation for shrinkage;\n\n**(7)** require a payment from a supplier for wastage, or for the deterioration or loss of products on the covered buyer's premises or after ownership has passed to the covered buyer, except for wastage, deterioration, or loss caused by the supplier's negligence or default as the supply agreement expressly and unambiguously defines it;\n\n**(8)** require a payment from a supplier as a condition of retaining a listing, a stocking, a shelf position, or an allocation of shelf space the supplier's products already have, other than a payment in relation to a promotion;\n\n**(9)** require a payment from a supplier for the cost of examining or resolving a consumer complaint, except a payment not exceeding the retail price of the product where the covered buyer has established on reasonable grounds that the complaint is attributable to the supplier's negligence or default;\n\n**(10)** require a supplier to obtain goods, services, or property from a third party from which the covered buyer receives a payment, unless the supplier's alternative source fails the objective quality standards the covered buyer applies to that supplier or charges more than a third party the covered buyer recommends;\n\n**(11)** refuse to confirm in writing, on a supplier's request, the terms of a supply agreement;\n\n**(12)** delist a supplier other than for a genuine commercial reason, or without giving the supplier reasonable notice of the decision with written reasons that state the facts concerning the supplier's products, prices, volumes, or performance on which the decision rests, informing the supplier of the review under \u00a7 4-6(c), and allowing the supplier that review before the delisting takes effect \u2014 and the supplier's exercise of a right under this title or under the supply agreement, and the covered buyer's own failure to perform a duty under this title, is not a genuine commercial reason; or\n\n**(13)** order products at a promotional price in a quantity it does not sell at the promotional price without paying the supplier the difference between the promotional and non-promotional wholesale price on the excess.\n\n**Relationship to the Perishable Agricultural Commodities Act, 1930.** As to a perishable agricultural commodity within the meaning of section 1(b)(4) of that Act (7 U.S.C. 499a(b)(4)), nothing in paragraph (3) extends a period for payment that section 2(4) of that Act (7 U.S.C. 499b(4)) or a regulation under it requires, and the shorter period governs; an agreement to a period for payment longer than thirty days is a term a covered buyer may not require under paragraph (3), whatever section 46.2(aa)(11) of title 7, Code of Federal Regulations, permits the parties to agree; and the last sentence of section 2(4) of that Act, concerning collateral fees and expenses, does not limit paragraphs (5), (6), (7), or (8) or \u00a7 4-3, and its omission from this title is a determination of Congress. Nothing in this title affects the trust under section 5(c) of that Act (7 U.S.C. 499e(c))."},{"anchor":"sec-4-3","heading":"\u00a7 4-3. Practices lawful only where stated in advance and published.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 GROCERY SUPPLY PRACTICES","body":"**(a)** A covered buyer shall not require any of the following of a supplier, and a supplier is not bound to any of them, unless the supply agreement stated it in clear and unambiguous terms before the products to which it relates were ordered; the covered buyer gave the supplier, on request, a written estimate of the amount per unit or in total and the basis of the estimate; and the covered buyer had published the standard terms on which it seeks the practice under \u00a7 4-7(a):\n\n**(1)** the return of unsold products without payment for them or for their disposal;\n\n**(2)** a payment as a condition of listing, stocking, or displaying a product not stocked by the covered buyer in the preceding 365 days, or of better positioning or an increased allocation of shelf space;\n\n**(3)** bearing any part of the cost of a discount in a promotion, where the covered buyer specified the period of the promotion and the quantity expected to be ordered at the discounted price before it began;\n\n**(4)** a payment for the covered buyer's advertising or marketing of the supplier's products;\n\n**(5)** a payment for the cost of staff fitting out premises for the sale of the supplier's products;\n\n**(6)** a contribution to the covered buyer's costs of buyer visits, artwork or packaging design, consumer or market research, the opening or refurbishing of a store, or hospitality for the covered buyer's staff; or\n\n**(7)** a significant change to the supplier's supply chain procedures during the term of a supply agreement, unless the covered buyer gave reasonable notice in writing or fully compensated the supplier for the net cost of not having done so.\n\n**(b)** A payment or practice under subsection (a) that a covered buyer requires without satisfying that subsection is a payment required in violation of \u00a7 4-2(5).\n\n**(c)** Nothing in subsection (a) permits a payment, allowance, service, or facility that section 2(d) or 2(e) of the Clayton Act (15 U.S.C. 13(d), (e)) prohibits, and a covered buyer that requires a supplier to make a payment or furnish a service that the supplier does not make available to the covered buyer's competitors on proportionally equal terms requires a payment not related to the sale of the supplier's products within \u00a7 4-2(5)."},{"anchor":"sec-4-4","heading":"\u00a7 4-4. Written terms; records.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 GROCERY SUPPLY PRACTICES","body":"**(a)** A covered buyer shall not enter into or perform a supply agreement unless every term of it is recorded in writing and the supplier has a copy; and where a subsequent arrangement under a supply agreement is agreed orally, the covered buyer shall confirm its terms in writing to the supplier within three business days.\n\n**(b)** A covered buyer shall retain the written terms of every supply agreement and every arrangement under it for three years after it ends, and shall provide them to the supplier to which they relate on request.\n\n**(c)** Where a covered buyer has not recorded a term, an arrangement, or a variation as this section requires, the supplier's written account of it is presumed to state its content in any proceeding under this Act, and the presumption is rebutted only by a writing made at the time of the term, arrangement, or variation, and not by testimony."},{"anchor":"sec-4-5","heading":"\u00a7 4-5. Retaliation.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 GROCERY SUPPLY PRACTICES","body":"**(a)** A covered buyer shall not threaten, carry out, or cause or permit an affiliate or intermediary to carry out, commercial retaliation against a supplier \u2014 including delisting, a reduction in orders, a worsening of terms, a delay in payment, or an exclusion from a promotion \u2014 because the supplier has exercised or attempted to exercise a right under this title or under a supply agreement, has complained to the covered buyer or to any officer of the United States or of a State, has responded to a survey under \u00a7 4-7, has cooperated with any investigation or proceeding, or has joined, formed, or assisted an association of suppliers. Treatment is retaliation if it would tend to deter a reasonable supplier from doing a thing stated in this subsection.\n\n**(b)** Section 7-4 governs causation, defenses, and relief."},{"anchor":"sec-4-6","heading":"\u00a7 4-6. Compliance officer; review; annual report.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 GROCERY SUPPLY PRACTICES","body":"**(a)** Each covered buyer shall designate an employee as its supply practices compliance officer, who shall be independent of, and not managed by, any employee involved in buying agricultural and food products; shall be provided the resources and access necessary to the role; shall be available to every supplier as a point of contact concerning this title; and shall be identified to every supplier and published.\n\n**(b)** Each covered buyer shall identify to every supplier a senior buyer, who manages or is senior to the buyer responsible for that supplier and who shall, on the supplier's written request, review any decision made concerning the supplier in relation to this title.\n\n**(c)** Before a delisting takes effect, the supplier is entitled to the review under subsection (b) and to an interview with the compliance officer concerning the decision.\n\n**(d)** Within four months after the end of each fiscal year, each covered buyer shall file with the Secretary of Agriculture, and publish, a report approved by the chair of its audit committee, or by its chief executive where it has none, stating every instance in that year in which a supplier identified a breach or alleged breach of this title and what was done; every dispute with a supplier concerning this title and its outcome; the training given to its buying employees concerning this title; and the results for that covered buyer of the survey under \u00a7 4-7(b). The report is sworn, and section 1001 of title 18, United States Code, applies to it."},{"anchor":"sec-4-7","heading":"\u00a7 4-7. Publication; the supplier survey.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 GROCERY SUPPLY PRACTICES","body":"**(a)** Each covered buyer shall publish, and keep current, the standard terms on which it seeks any practice under \u00a7 4-3(a), including every rate, fee, and allowance.\n\n**(b)** For each calendar year, the Secretary of Agriculture, acting through the National Agricultural Statistics Service, shall conduct, and publish by the first day of the seventh month after its end, an anonymous survey of the suppliers to each covered buyer, with a sample sufficient to report each covered buyer separately, reporting for each covered buyer the share of responding suppliers that experienced each practice stated in \u00a7\u00a7 4-2 and 4-3 in that year, and the share that refrained from complaining for fear of retaliation. The survey is conducted, and every response is protected, under subchapter III of chapter 35 of title 44, United States Code; the identity of a responding supplier is not disclosed to the covered buyer or to any person outside the Service; a supplier may respond through an association; and every covered buyer shall provide the Service, on request, a list of its suppliers for the purpose of the survey.\n\n**(c)** Where the Secretary has not published the survey by the deadline in subsection (b), any association of suppliers may conduct and publish a survey meeting that subsection's terms, and a survey so published governs for purposes of \u00a7 4-6(d) until the Secretary publishes.\n\n**(d)** A supplier, and any association of suppliers, is aggrieved under \u00a7 7-1 by a failure to publish under this section, and the court shall order publication. The denial of a publication under this section to a supplier is an injury to the supplier's ability to determine whether it has a claim under this title and to invoke \u00a7\u00a7 4-6 and 7-1, and the supplier need show no other injury."},{"anchor":"sec-4-8","heading":"\u00a7 4-8. No contracting out; law governing.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 GROCERY SUPPLY PRACTICES","body":"**(a)** A term of any supply agreement or other instrument, whenever entered, that waives, avoids, restricts, limits, or conditions a duty under this title, or a supplier's right under it, is void and unenforceable.\n\n**(b)** This title applies to every supply agreement within its scope regardless of the law that would otherwise govern the agreement, and every supply agreement shall be treated as incorporating this title as an enforceable term.\n\n**(c)** Where a covered buyer requires an intermediary to impose on an agricultural producer a term or practice this title prohibits the covered buyer from imposing on a supplier, the covered buyer violates this title as to the producer, and the producer is aggrieved under \u00a7 7-1."},{"anchor":"sec-4-9","heading":"\u00a7 4-9. Examination.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 GROCERY SUPPLY PRACTICES","body":"**(a)** The Secretary of Agriculture, acting through the Agricultural Marketing Service, shall examine each covered buyer's compliance with this title not less than once in each period set in the schedule to this Act, by comparing the covered buyer's reports under \u00a7 4-6(d) for the period against the survey under \u00a7 4-7(b), the complaints the Secretary has received concerning the covered buyer, and the covered buyer's records under \u00a7 4-4, which the covered buyer shall produce on request; and shall publish within ninety days after the examination concludes a finding whether the reports and the records are consistent with the survey and the complaints, and every inconsistency found. The duty is ministerial.\n\n**(b)** Where the Secretary has not published findings for a covered buyer within the period, the covered buyer's most recent report under \u00a7 4-6(d) is published with a statement that it has not been examined, and the survey under \u00a7 4-7(b) governs for that buyer."},{"anchor":"sec-4-10","heading":"\u00a7 4-10. Severability within this title.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 GROCERY SUPPLY PRACTICES","body":"Each paragraph of \u00a7 4-2 is severable from every other. Section 4-3 is severable from \u00a7 4-2, and \u00a7 4-8(c) from the remainder of \u00a7 4-8. The invalidity of any provision of this title does not affect any other provision of this title or of this Act, and no court shall treat the provisions of this title as an integrated scheme."},{"anchor":"sec-5-1","heading":"\u00a7 5-1. Definitions for this title.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 ACQUISITIONS IN THE FOOD SUPPLY CHAIN","body":"**(a)** **Covered food market** means a line of commerce, in any section of the country, in \u2014 the slaughter or processing of livestock or poultry; the manufacture or sale of seed, crop protection chemicals, or fertilizer; the merchandising, storage, or export of grain or oilseeds; the manufacture or sale of agricultural equipment; the processing, manufacture, or distribution of food for human consumption; or the retail sale of groceries.\n\n**(b)** **Acquisition** has the meaning it has in section 7 of the Clayton Act (15 U.S.C. 18), and **person**, for purposes of aggregation under this title, includes every affiliate of the person as determined under section 7A(b)(3)(B) of that Act (15 U.S.C. 18a(b)(3)(B)).\n\n**(c)** **Four-firm concentration ratio** and **Herfindahl-Hirschman index** are computed for a covered food market from the shares of the persons participating in it, by the method the Federal Trade Commission and the Attorney General publish for the review of mergers as in effect on January 1, 2025, whether or not that publication remains in effect; and where no such method is in effect, by the method any party demonstrates from the publicly available data.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE V \u2014 ACQUISITIONS IN THE FOOD SUPPLY CHAIN","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-5-2","heading":"\u00a7 5-2. Presumption.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 ACQUISITIONS IN THE FOOD SUPPLY CHAIN","body":"**(a)** In an action under section 7 of the Clayton Act (15 U.S.C. 18), or under section 15 or 16 of that Act (15 U.S.C. 25, 26) to enforce section 7, an acquisition is presumed to have the effect of substantially lessening competition, and to tend to create a monopoly, in a covered food market where, after the acquisition, in that market \u2014\n\n**(1)** the acquiring person's share exceeds the percentage set in the schedule to this Act;\n\n**(2)** the four-firm concentration ratio exceeds the percentage set in the schedule to this Act; or\n\n**(3)** the Herfindahl-Hirschman index exceeds the level set in the schedule to this Act and the acquisition increases it by more than the amount set in that schedule.\n\n**(b)** The presumption is rebutted only by evidence clearly showing that the acquisition is not likely to have such effects, and the acquiring person bears that burden. A showing that the acquisition would produce efficiencies does not rebut the presumption unless the person establishes, by the same standard, that the efficiencies could not be achieved without the acquisition and would be passed on in the covered food market.\n\n**(c)** This section supplements section 7 of the Clayton Act and does not limit any showing that may be made without the presumption. Each threshold in subsection (a) is a step: at or below it the presumption does not arise, above it the presumption arises in full, and the schedule states no intermediate effect."},{"anchor":"sec-5-3","heading":"\u00a7 5-3. Serial acquisitions aggregate for notification.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 ACQUISITIONS IN THE FOOD SUPPLY CHAIN","body":"Section 7A of the Clayton Act (15 U.S.C. 18a) is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(l) Aggregation in covered food markets.** For purposes of subsection (a)(2), where the acquiring person has, within the period set in the schedule to the Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act ending on the date of an acquisition, acquired voting securities or assets of one or more other persons participating in a covered food market within the same category stated in section 5-1(a) of that Act, the acquiring person is treated as holding, as a result of the acquisition, the aggregate of the voting securities and assets so acquired in that period together with those acquired in the acquisition, and the notification required by subsection (a) is required accordingly. An acquisition that becomes notifiable by reason of this subsection is notifiable notwithstanding subsection (c)(1)."},{"anchor":"sec-5-4","heading":"\u00a7 5-4. Actions.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 ACQUISITIONS IN THE FOOD SUPPLY CHAIN","body":"An action under this title, or under section 7, 15, or 16 of the Clayton Act (15 U.S.C. 18, 25, 26) with respect to an acquisition in a covered food market, is an action under this Act for purposes of \u00a7 7-2 and \u00a7 7-5. Nothing in this title conditions or limits an action a State may bring under section 4C or 16 of that Act (15 U.S.C. 15c, 26)."},{"anchor":"sec-5-5","heading":"\u00a7 5-5. Severability within this title.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 ACQUISITIONS IN THE FOOD SUPPLY CHAIN","body":"Section 5-3 is severable from \u00a7 5-2, and operates whether or not \u00a7 5-2 is in force. The invalidity of any threshold in \u00a7 5-2(a) leaves every other threshold in force. The invalidity of any provision of this title does not affect any other provision of this title or of this Act, and no court shall treat the provisions of this title as an integrated scheme."},{"anchor":"sec-6-1","heading":"\u00a7 6-1. The claims \"Product of USA\" and \"Made in the USA.\"","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 ORIGIN OF MEAT AND POULTRY","body":"**(a) Single-ingredient products.** The claim \"Product of USA,\" the claim \"Made in the USA,\" and any claim, statement, symbol, or display of a flag conveying that a product is of United States origin, may be used on the label of a single-ingredient meat food product, poultry product, or egg product only where the product is derived from an animal born, raised, slaughtered, and processed in the United States.\n\n**(b) Multi-ingredient products.** Such a claim may be used on the label of a multi-ingredient product only where every ingredient in the product that is produced under Federal inspection is derived from an animal born, raised, slaughtered, and processed in the United States; every other ingredient, other than a spice or flavoring, is of domestic origin; and every preparation and processing step for the product occurred in the United States.\n\n**(c) Qualified claims.** A claim of United States origin other than those stated in subsections (a) and (b), and a claim of the origin of a product in a State, territory, or locality of the United States, may be used only where it states the preparation and processing steps that occurred in the United States, State, territory, or locality on which the claim rests, and is truthful and not misleading.\n\n**(d) Misbranding.** A meat food product or poultry product bearing a claim that subsection (a), (b), or (c) does not permit shall be treated as bearing labeling that is false or misleading in a particular, and as misbranded, for purposes of section 1(n)(1) of the Federal Meat Inspection Act (21 U.S.C. 601(n)(1)) and section 4(h)(1) of the Poultry Products Inspection Act (21 U.S.C. 453(h)(1)) respectively; and an egg product bearing such a claim shall be treated as not labeled in accordance with the regulations under section 7 of the Egg Products Inspection Act (21 U.S.C. 1036), and as misbranded, for purposes of section 4(l) of that Act (21 U.S.C. 1033(l)). Every remedy for misbranding under those Acts applies to a product so treated.\n\n**(e) Records.** An establishment or facility using a claim under this section shall maintain, and produce on request to the Secretary of Agriculture and to a plaintiff in an action under \u00a7 7-1, records sufficient to establish that each animal and ingredient meets the condition on which the claim rests, in the manner of section 412.3(f) and (g) of title 9, Code of Federal Regulations, as in effect on January 1, 2026, whether or not that section remains in effect. A claim for which such records are not maintained does not satisfy this section.\n\n**(f) Nothing here requires a statement of origin.** This section conditions a claim voluntarily made. It does not require a statement of the country of origin of any product, and it does not amend section 282 of the Agricultural Marketing Act of 1946 (7 U.S.C. 1638a). A designation of country of origin that section 282 of that Act requires a retailer to make, in the form that section prescribes, is not a claim within subsection (a), (b), or (c); every other claim, statement, symbol, or flag conveying United States origin on such a product is.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VI \u2014 ORIGIN OF MEAT AND POULTRY","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-6-2","heading":"\u00a7 6-2. No weakening by rule.","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 ORIGIN OF MEAT AND POULTRY","body":"The conditions stated in \u00a7 6-1 operate of their own force. Section 412.3 of title 9, Code of Federal Regulations, as in effect on January 1, 2026, continues to govern the approval of labels bearing the claims \u00a7 6-1 addresses whether or not that section remains in effect, and no rule, guidance, or approval of the Secretary of Agriculture may permit a claim that \u00a7 6-1 does not permit."},{"anchor":"sec-6-3","heading":"\u00a7 6-3. Severability within this title.","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 ORIGIN OF MEAT AND POULTRY","body":"Subsections (a), (b), and (c) of \u00a7 6-1 are severable from one another. The invalidity of any provision of this title does not affect any other provision of this title or of this Act, and no court shall treat the provisions of this title as an integrated scheme."},{"anchor":"sec-7-1","heading":"\u00a7 7-1. Civil action.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) The action.** A person aggrieved by a violation of this Act may bring a civil action against the person that committed it for declaratory and injunctive relief; for the person's actual damages; for the amount set in the schedule to this Act for each violation, or, where the violation continues, for each day it continues; and for reasonable attorney's fees, expert fees, and costs, which the court shall award to a prevailing plaintiff. A person is **aggrieved** by a violation if the person is an owner, independent repair provider, grower, producer, supplier, or purchaser to whom a duty under this Act ran and was not performed, or a competitor of the violator that has complied with the duty the violator breached.\n\n**(b) Expressly conferred.** The right and the action stated in this section are conferred expressly by this section, and no presumption against the creation of a private right or a private remedy applies to it.\n\n**(c) Pleading.** A complaint states a claim under this section where it alleges facts which, if true, would establish that the defendant is subject to a duty under this Act and did not perform it. No heightened standard of pleading applies, and knowledge, intention, and motive may be alleged generally and proved by circumstantial evidence.\n\n**(d) Predicate facts.** The nonperformance of a duty under this Act constitutes irreparable injury for which there is no adequate remedy at law as to relief against a continuing violation, and the balance of equities and the public interest favor relief. The cessation, suspension, or expiration of a challenged practice does not render an action moot where the defendant remains free to resume it, and a defendant asserting mootness bears the burden of establishing that the practice cannot reasonably be expected to recur.\n\n**(e) No preconditions.** Neither exhaustion of any administrative remedy, nor notice to any officer or agency, nor any finding, determination, or action by any agency, is a condition of an action under this section, and no statute establishing an administrative or remedial scheme shall be construed to channel, preclude, or displace a claim under this Act unless it does so by express reference to this Act.\n\n**(f) No agency assignment.** Nothing in this Act authorizes a person to sue as an assignee, in whole or in part, of a claim of the United States.\n\n**(g) Limitations.** An action under this section may be brought within the period set in the schedule to this Act after the later of the violation or the plaintiff's discovery of it.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-7-2","heading":"\u00a7 7-2. Forum; arbitration.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Concurrent jurisdiction; removal barred.** **(1)** An action under this Act may be brought in a district court of the United States or in any State court of competent jurisdiction. **(2)** Such an action brought in a State court may not be removed to a district court of the United States. **(3)** A State court shall not decline to hear such an action on the ground that it arises under Federal law, nor dismiss it on the ground that the plaintiff would lack standing in a court of the United States.\n\n**(b) Predispute arbitration and joint-action waivers.** **(1)** Notwithstanding chapter 1 of title 9, United States Code, at the election of the plaintiff, or of the named representative of a class or collective action, no predispute arbitration agreement and no predispute joint-action waiver is valid or enforceable with respect to the case relating to a claim under this Act, and **case** means the entire civil proceeding, not the claim alone. The election may be made after the dispute arises, and a term purporting to require the election earlier, or to waive it, is void. **(2)** A court shall not compel arbitration of any part of such a case unless it first determines that this subsection does not apply, notwithstanding any delegation of that determination to an arbitrator and notwithstanding any incorporation of arbitral rules. **(3)** The election is available where the claim is pleaded nonfrivolously, and no more is required to invoke it. **(4)** Where any provision of an agreement is unenforceable under this subsection, the agreement is unenforceable in its entirety as to that case, notwithstanding any severability term in it. **(5)** An agreement is **predispute** if entered before the conduct giving rise to the claim occurred, whenever the claim is filed. **(6)** This subsection applies to an agreement entered before, on, or after the date of enactment.\n\n**(c) Forum and law.** A term selecting the forum or the law of another jurisdiction is void to the extent it would apply to a claim under this Act.\n\n**(d) Conforming amendment.** Section 1445 of title 28, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **\"(e)** A civil action arising under the Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act may not be removed to any district court of the United States.\"\n\n**(e) The bar does not depend on the amendment.** Subsection (a)(2) operates according to its terms as an Act of Congress expressly providing otherwise within the meaning of section 1441(a) of title 28, United States Code, whether or not subsection (d) takes effect."},{"anchor":"sec-7-3","heading":"\u00a7 7-3. Enforcement by a State; by the United States.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** The attorney general of a State may bring a civil action for the relief available under \u00a7 7-1 on behalf of the residents of that State, and in the name of the State for the civil penalty set in the schedule to this Act for each violation, which a district court determines. This subsection supplements \u00a7 7-1 and is not a precondition of it.\n\n**(b)** The Attorney General, the Secretary of Agriculture, and the Federal Trade Commission may each bring a civil action in a district court of the United States for the relief available under subsection (a). No action, finding, rule, guidance, or inaction of any of them is a condition of, a defense to, or evidence in an action under \u00a7 7-1 or subsection (a), and none of them may compromise, release, or bar a claim of any other person.\n\n**(c)** Every civil penalty under this Act is determined by a district court. Nothing in this Act authorizes the assessment of a penalty by any agency."},{"anchor":"sec-7-4","heading":"\u00a7 7-4. Retaliation \u2014 causation and defenses.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** Wherever this Act prohibits retaliation, less favorable treatment, or an adverse action by reason of a protected thing, a violation is established where the protected thing was a contributing factor in the treatment or action, and no other causal standard applies. It is a defense only that the same treatment or action would have been taken had the protected thing not occurred, and the defendant bears the burden of establishing that defense by clear and convincing evidence.\n\n**(b)** A person who prevails on a retaliation claim under this Act recovers, in addition to the relief under \u00a7 7-1, the relief necessary to be made whole, including restoration of the relationship, the terms, the placement, the listing, or the access that was withdrawn or degraded."},{"anchor":"sec-7-5","heading":"\u00a7 7-5. Defenses and construction.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Exclusive.** The defenses, immunities, privileges, exceptions, and limitations on liability available in an action under this Act are those and only those expressly stated in this Act.\n\n**(b) No common-law supplement.** The common law of the United States or of any State supplies no defense, immunity, or limitation. No defense is to be inferred from the omission of any subject from this Act, and the absence of a provision addressing a defense recognized at common law or under any other statute is a determination of Congress that it is unavailable.\n\n**(c) No reconstitution.** A defense abolished or omitted may not be reintroduced as an element of the plaintiff's case, a pleading requirement, a standard of proof, a component of the merits, a limitation on remedy, or a prudential limitation on jurisdiction. In particular, no provision of this Act requires a plaintiff to plead or prove injury to competition, market power, a relevant market, intent, or an anticompetitive effect, except where the provision states that element in terms.\n\n**(d) Statutory, not implied.** Each cause of action created by this Act is created by this Act. No court may decline to entertain it on the ground that special factors counsel hesitation, that an alternative remedial structure exists, that the context is new, or that Congress is better positioned to weigh the consequences.\n\n**(e) Canons addressed.** Congress has considered and expressly displaces, to the extent this Act so provides, any presumption against displacement of State law, any presumption against the creation of a private remedy, any presumption that a Federal claim is removable to a Federal forum, and, as to \u00a7\u00a7 1-4(a), 2-6, 7-2(b), and 8-4, any presumption against the application of a statute to an agreement entered before its enactment. No such presumption applies to any provision of this Act.\n\n**(f) Remedial.** This Act is remedial and shall be construed to give effect to its remedial purpose. **A violation of a provision of another Act that this Act adds or amends shall be treated as a violation of this Act for purposes of this title, without prejudice to any remedy under that other Act.** The duties of this Act operate on the defaults this Act supplies, without any rulemaking or other agency action, and no agency may exempt any person, class, or transaction from any of them.\n\n**(g) Character of amounts received.** An amount received under this Act that recovers a payment made, a charge imposed, or an amount withheld in violation of this Act has the character, for purposes of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, of the payment, charge, or amount it recovers. Any other amount received under \u00a7 7-1 is includible in gross income but is not wages and is not net earnings from self-employment. An amount paid as the per-violation amount under \u00a7 7-1(a) or under section 308(a) of the Packers and Stockyards Act, 1921, as amended by \u00a7 2-5, and a civil penalty under \u00a7 7-3 or under any provision this Act amends, is not deductible under any provision of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, and section 162(f)(2) of that Code does not apply to it."},{"anchor":"sec-7-6","heading":"\u00a7 7-6. Conforming amendment \u2014 treatment of a fee award.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** Section 62(e) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended by adding at the end the following new paragraph:\n\n> **(22)** The Farmer Freedom and Lower Cost Groceries Act, and any provision of another Act that that Act adds or amends.\n\n**(b)** Subsection (a) applies to amounts paid in taxable years ending after the date of enactment."},{"anchor":"sec-8-1","heading":"\u00a7 8-1. Relationship to other law.","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a)** This Act does not preempt, displace, or limit a law of a State or an Indian Tribe that provides a person a right, a remedy, or a protection greater than this Act, and such a law governs to that extent. Nothing in this Act dictates the content of Tribal law; a Tribal government joins each protection and recognition on election and is bound only as to functions voluntarily assumed.\n\n**(b)** This Act supplements, and does not limit, the antitrust laws as defined in section 1 of the Clayton Act (15 U.S.C. 12), the Packers and Stockyards Act, 1921, the Federal Trade Commission Act, and every other Federal law, and no provision of this Act shall be construed as a determination that conduct it does not reach is lawful under any of them.\n\n**(c)** A duty stated in this Act applies to any other authority invoked to reach substantially the same result, regardless of form, title, or characterization, and a person that would reach a result this Act prohibits by another route violates this Act.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VIII \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-8-2","heading":"\u00a7 8-2. Resources and continuity of operations.","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"There are permanently appropriated such sums as are necessary for the publication, survey, and examination functions this Act assigns to any officer. A lapse in appropriations suspends no duty, no clock, and no right under this Act, none of which depends on any appropriation. **The officers and employees necessary to perform the publication, survey, and examination functions this Act assigns to any officer shall be designated as excepted from furlough during a lapse in appropriations, identified as such in the published shutdown plan of the agency concerned.**"},{"anchor":"sec-8-3","heading":"\u00a7 8-3. Severability and anti-aggregation.","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"If any provision of this Act, or any application of a provision, is held invalid, every other provision and application stands. No court shall treat the provisions of this Act, or of this Act together with any other Act, as an integrated scheme whose parts stand or fall together. Each title is severable from every other title; within each title, the severability stated in that title governs; and the invalidity of any application of this Act to an agreement entered before the date of enactment does not affect its application to any agreement entered after."},{"anchor":"sec-8-4","heading":"\u00a7 8-4. Effective dates.","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a)** This Act takes effect on the date of enactment, except as this section provides.\n\n**(b)** Title I applies to repair resources and to conduct from the first day of the seventh month after enactment, except that \u00a7 1-6(b) applies from the date of enactment; and Title I applies to a contract, license, warranty, agreement, or arrangement whenever entered.\n\n**(c)** Title II applies to conduct occurring after the date of enactment; \u00a7 2-6 applies to any contract whenever entered.\n\n**(d)** Title V applies to an acquisition agreed to after the date of enactment; \u00a7 5-3 counts acquisitions made before the date of enactment toward the aggregation period.\n\n**(e)** Title VI applies to a product labeled on or after the first day of the seventh month after enactment.\n\n**(f)** Title III applies from the first day of the seventh month after enactment, except that \u00a7 3-2(c) governs contracts in force on the date of enactment and \u00a7 3-3 applies to the first fiscal year ending after enactment.\n\n**(g)** Title IV applies to a supply agreement entered, renewed, or extended after the first day of the seventh month after enactment, and to every supply agreement from the first day of the nineteenth month after enactment; \u00a7\u00a7 4-6 and 4-7 apply from the first day of the seventh month after enactment."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"1. **Amount per violation, private action** (\u00a7 7-1(a)): [$___ per violation; $___ per day of a continuing violation]\n2. **Limitations period** (\u00a7 7-1(g)): [___ years]\n3. **Civil penalty per violation, action by a State or the United States** (\u00a7 7-3(a)): [$___, inflation-indexed]\n4. **Amount per violation, Packers and Stockyards Act \u00a7 308(a) as amended** (\u00a7 2-5): [$___ per violation]\n5. **Civil penalty per violation, Packers and Stockyards Act \u00a7\u00a7 203(b), 312(b), 411(b) as amended** (\u00a7 2-7): [$___, inflation-indexed]\n6. **Presumption thresholds** (\u00a7 5-2(a)): [acquiring person's post-acquisition share: ___ percent; four-firm concentration ratio: ___ percent; Herfindahl-Hirschman index level: ___ and increase: ___]\n7. **Aggregation period for serial acquisitions** (\u00a7 5-3): [___ years]\n8. **Additional capital investment threshold, per structure** (\u00a7 2-4, Act \u00a7 212(a)): [$___]\n9. **Limit on ranked payments as a share of total gross payments at a complex** (\u00a7 2-4, Act \u00a7 212(b)): [___ percent]\n10. **Amount for a false lobbying certification** (\u00a7 3-2(a-0)): [$___]\n11. **Expenditure and compensation publication thresholds** (\u00a7 3-3(a)): [$___ per expenditure; $___ per employee]\n12. **Covered buyer threshold** (\u00a7 4-1(b)): [$40,000,000 in annual retail sales or purchases for resale of agricultural and food products]\n13. **Minimum notice period** (\u00a7 4-1(j)): [___ days]\n14. **Examination period** (\u00a7 4-9(a)): [___ years]"}],"asOf":"2026-11-18"}}}};
